  "source": {
    "full": "Ralph Engel and Mary Engel, Diccionario zoque de Copainalá, with a grammar (Summer Institute of Linguistics, 1981); Instituto Nacional de Lenguas Indígenas, Otejaye'is 'yanhki'mkuy / Norma de escritura de la lengua otetzame (zoque), written here with ts instead of tz. See also: Engel, Diccionario zoque de Francisco León (SIL, 1987)."
  },
  "about": [
    "Zoque is a language of the Mixe-Zoquean family, related to Mixe and Popoluca. It is spoken in northwestern Chiapas, in towns such as Copainalá, Tecpatán, Ocotepec, Chapultenango, Francisco León and Tapalapa, and also in the Chimalapas of Oaxaca.",
    "Its speakers call it otetsame or ote, and in Copainalá and Tecpatán tsunitsame or tsuni. The eruption of the Chichonal volcano in 1982 displaced many Zoque communities and scattered their speakers.",
    "The Zoque verb marks the person twice: with prefixes that change the first consonant of the root (tsäjk → tsyäjku «he/she did it») and with pronouns attached at the end (poyu'äj «I ran»). It tells apart «we» with and without the listener."
  ],
  "orthography": {
    "intro": "We use the alphabet of INALI's writing norm (the Nenhejaye), with ts instead of tz: 6 vowels and 14 consonants. The apostrophe (') is the glottal stop, a short break in the voice, and nh sounds like the ng in «sing».",
