    ],
    "notes": [
      "The glottal stop changes the meaning: ju'ki «vulture», juki «cigarette». It isn't written at the start of a word that begins with a vowel: ane, not 'ane.",
      "After m, n and nh, the consonants p, t, ts and k sound voiced (mpama sounds like «mbama»), but they are written voiceless.",
      "The y of the 3rd person slips in after the first consonant: tsat → tsyat; ty, tsy, sy and ny sound like a single palatal consonant.",
      "Pronunciation is shown in brackets with the International Phonetic Alphabet (IPA)."
    ]
  },
  "grammar": {
    "intro": "A Zoque verb is built with a person prefix, the root, a plural suffix, a tense suffix and a pronoun attached at the end. Many tenses are made with an auxiliary verb in front.",
    "blocks": [
      {
        "items": [
          {
            "slot": "Prefix",
            "description": "N- 1st person, Ny- 2nd, y- 3rd; they change the first consonant of the root."
          },
          {
            "slot": "Root",
            "description": "The verb."
          },
          {
            "slot": "Plural",
            "description": "-tam- you all and inclusive we, -yaj- they."
          },
          {
            "slot": "Tense",
            "description": "-pa unfinished, -u finished; -A order, -I negation."
          },
          {
            "slot": "Person",
            "description": "-'äj / -'äs I, -mij / -mis you and you all, -täj / -täs we, -tä we including the listener."
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "The persons",
        "description": "Intransitive verbs use -'äj and -mij; transitive ones, -'äs and -mis. The prefixes depend on the type of verb.",
        "columns": [
          "Person",
          "Intransitive",
          "Transitive",
          "Example"
        ],
        "rows": [
          {
            "label": "I"
          },
          {
            "label": "you"
          },
          {
            "label": "he, she"
          },
          {
            "label": "we"
          },
          {
            "label": "we (with you)"
          },
          {
            "label": "you all"
          },
          {
            "label": "they"
          }
        ],
        "note": "N- assimilates to the next consonant: m before p (mpoyu), n before t and ts, nh before k (nhkyenu)."
      },
      {
        "title": "Sound changes",
        "description": "When the pieces join, the sounds adjust.",
        "items": [
          {
            "tag": "y moves",
            "name": "Metathesis",
            "description": "A root ending in y moves it after the next consonant.",
            "examples": [
              {
                "meaning": "he/she runs"
              },
              {
                "meaning": "he/she buys it"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "tag": "j is lost",
            "name": "Reduction",
            "description": "jC + another consonant loses the j.",
            "examples": [
              {
                "meaning": "he/she does"
              },
              {
                "meaning": "you all sweep"
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "tag": "-A / -I",
            "name": "Vowel harmony",
            "description": "The vowel of the order and of the negation copies the root's.",
            "examples": [
              {
                "meaning": "do it!"
              },
              {
                "meaning": "he/she doesn't run"
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "title": "Auxiliaries",
        "description": "The auxiliary takes the pronoun; the main verb, the person prefix and -u.",
